Category: Reliability / Developer Experience. Severity: Medium. Location: frontend/src/app/page.tsx, frontend/src/app/auth/login/page.tsx, frontend/src/app/auth/register/page.tsx. Problem: the homepage throws if the backend fetch fails, and the login/register pages are inert forms with no submission logic, validation, or state handling. Why it matters: a transient API outage can take down the landing page, and the auth UI currently communicates functionality that does not exist. Suggested solution: add fallback rendering or route-level error handling, wire forms to real endpoints, and add validation and submit-state management. Estimated impact: medium; this hurts perceived reliability and makes the product look unfinished.

Category: Code Quality. Severity: Low to Medium. Location: backend/app/articles/service.py, backend/app/articles/repository.py. Problem: the service layer is only a pass-through that remaps ORM fields to dicts one-for-one, so it adds indirection without adding business logic. Why it matters: unnecessary layers are not free; they create more places for drift, more code to read, and more temptation to add logic in the wrong place later. Suggested solution: either return ORM objects directly and let the response model serialize them, or keep a service layer only where real business rules exist. Estimated impact: low to medium; this is technical clutter, not a production blocker.

Category: Testing. Severity: High. Location: Repo-wide. Problem: I found no test files at all, so there is no unit, integration, or regression coverage for auth, ingestion, repository behavior, or the API contract. Why it matters: the current schema mismatch, import brittleness, and missing auth enforcement are exactly the sort of defects tests should catch before review. Suggested solution: add unit tests for auth helpers and ingestion normalization, repository tests for article ordering and dedup behavior, and at least one API integration test for the articles endpoint. Estimated impact: high on maintainability; without tests this codebase will become risky to change very quickly.

Category: DevOps / Observability. Severity: Medium. Location: Repo-wide, especially backend/app/main.py, backend/app/ingestion/scheduler.py, and the absence of deployment files. Problem: I found no Dockerfile, compose file, or CI workflow, and the runtime uses print statements instead of structured logging or health endpoints. Why it matters: deployment, debugging, and production support will all be manual and fragile; scaling this to multiple environments will be painful. Suggested solution: add containerization, CI for lint/test/build, structured logs, and readiness/health endpoints. Estimated impact: medium to high; this is a readiness gap.

Category: Dependencies / Supply Chain. Severity: Medium. Location: backend/requirements.txt. Problem: the dependency list is bloated with notebook, visualization, ML, and agent tooling packages that the application code does not use. Why it matters: every extra package expands the attack surface, slows installs, complicates upgrades, and makes vulnerability management noisier. Suggested solution: split production requirements from dev/notebook extras and keep the runtime manifest minimal. Estimated impact: medium; this is operational drag and supply-chain risk.